= Introduction 
The electric vehicle (EV) market has experienced substantial growth in recent years. As of 2026, EVs represent approximately 20% of the Danish automotive population @Mobility. Current forecasts suggest this figure will rise to 45% by 2030 @KEFM2025. As EV adoption grows, charging stations may see increasing congestion of vehicles, especially along major highways and at peak commuting hours.

In the absence of a smart scheduling system, two primary inefficiencies can arise: drivers encounter increased wait times due to queuing, and the charging infrastructure suffers from sub-optimal, uneven utilization or overloading of the grid. These challenges are not purely a matter of deploying more chargers, as even a comprehensive network may suffer from poor load distribution if drivers consistently converge upon the same stations. Evaluating scheduling strategies directly in the real world is impractical, which motivates the use of simulation to explore how preference-aware, decentralized charging decisions might influence station congestion and utilization across a national network.

However, current state-of-the-art simulation models showcases a tradeoff between geographic scale and behavioral realism. Existing studies typically rely on deterministic transit fleets, validate algorithms on small artificial maps, or restrict complex event logic to highly localized zones. Consequently, a gap remains in evaluating preference-driven behavior of private EVs at a national scale. This paper attempts to bridge that gap by introducing a simulation environment that combines individual EV preferences, event-driven decision logic, and nationwide scale within a single framework.